00:05:14,761 --> 00:05:16,940
which we can take fully loaded combat soldiers

102
00:05:16,941 --> 00:05:19,440
and air assault missions onto the battlefield.

103
00:05:19,441 --> 00:05:22,740
And then we can turn around and pick up logistical supplies

104
00:05:22,741 --> 00:05:26,680
from sling-loading water pallets or MRE pallets

105
00:05:26,681 --> 00:05:29,660
or ammunition to help resupply soldiers.

106
00:05:29,661 --> 00:05:32,560
We can do medevac missions, which is picking up casualties

107
00:05:32,561 --> 00:05:35,220
on the battlefield, delivering to the medical treatment

108
00:05:35,221 --> 00:05:37,420
facilities, which has drastically increased

109
00:05:37,421 --> 00:05:39,420
the survivability on the battlefield.

110
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:41,560
Number one and number two battery, battery utility,

111
00:05:41,561 --> 00:05:46,560
The Blackhawk helicopter crew includes two pilots.

112
00:05:46,561 --> 00:05:51,560
To fly it, the pilots must use both hands and feet.

113
00:05:51,561 --> 00:05:55,560
The helicopter's superior maneuverability comes with complexity.

114
00:05:55,561 --> 00:05:58,560
The key to understanding how to fly a Blackhawk or any

115
00:05:58,561 --> 00:06:01,560
helicopter starts at the hub.

116
00:06:01,561 --> 00:06:04,560
The hub is a central portion of the rotor system.

117
00:06:04,561 --> 00:06:08,560
It's the part where all four rotor blades hook together.

118
00:06:08,561 --> 00:06:15,780
The hub or rotor disc tilts to give the helicopter directional flight and is controlled by the cyclic.

119
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:18,951
This is the cyclic.

120
00:06:18,952 --> 00:06:25,900
This provides us directional control, either at a hover or in a cruise flight mode.

121
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:31,860
With a cyclic, I'm actually tilting the rotor disc in the direction that I'm pushing the cyclic.

122
00:06:31,861 --> 00:06:35,900
So therefore, if I'm at a hover and I move the cyclic to the right,

123
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:38,720
then the aircraft rotor disk is going to tilt to the right,

124
00:06:38,820 --> 00:06:40,200
and I'm going to drift to the right.

125
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:44,780
If I push the cyclic forward, the rotor system is going to tilt forward,

126
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:46,620
the aircraft is going to go into a descent,

127
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:50,500
and I look outside and the houses on the ground are actually getting bigger

128
00:06:50,501 --> 00:06:52,120
because I'm getting closer to the ground.

129
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:55,560
To control the lift or thrust of the helicopter,

130
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:57,680
the pilot uses the collective.

131
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,620
The flight control that I have in my left hand here is called the collective.

132
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:05,860
And it's called the collective because it collectively puts

133
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:09,500
blade pitch angle into all four rotor blades at the same time.

134
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:11,360
The more pitch I want in the rotor blade,

135
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:13,700
the higher I pull the collective.

136
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,800
The pitch angle determines how fast the helicopter will lift

137
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:21,520
off the ground in a hover or how fast it will fly in flight.

138
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:24,520
A zero angle will produce zero lift.

139
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:26,060
As the pitch increases, the blades

140
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:30,460
will push more air downward, resulting in greater lift.

141
00:07:30,461 --> 00:07:33,120
Once in the air, the pilot pushes the cyclic forward

142
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:36,800
to start the helicopter in a forward direction.

143
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:40,100
RPA 069, we'll just slow down here in the orbit

144
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,600
and just let you catch up to us.

145
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:43,500
As the pilot flies the aircraft,

146
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:46,100
he uses the tail rotor to keep the nose of the helicopter

147
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:48,720
pointing forward.

148
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:51,820
The purpose of that tail rotor is to provide thrust

149
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,920
in the opposite direction of the main rotor turning

150
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:58,220
so that we can maintain our directional flight.

151
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:01,420
Since the main rotor system turns counterclockwise,

152
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:04,400
there's an equal and opposite reaction that forces the fuselage

153
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,600
clockwise.

154
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:07,900
The tail rotor counters that.

155
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,800
It works the same way as the main rotor,

156
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:12,100
in that its blades increase pitch to gain thrust.

157
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:17,100
To maintain a pitch that will keep the helicopter moving

158
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:21,480
forward, the pilot uses his feet to control the tail rotor.

159
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:24,840
If I push left pedal, then that causes the nose of the helicopter

160
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:27,340
to turn left.

161
00:08:27,341 --> 00:08:29,880
And then I use counter pressure on the opposite pedal

162
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:31,180
to stop that motion.

163
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:36,680
The Black Hawk has two crew chiefs

164
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:38,580
who are responsible for everything that happens

165
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:41,180
